/* ============================================================================
   permutations.epher -- ordered arrangements: npr(n, r) and the runners

   Counts ordered selections with the npr builtin (guide 1.13): the number
   of ways to pick and order r distinct objects out of n. The classic
   setting is the race: with 10 runners there are npr(10, 3) = 720 ways the
   gold, silver and bronze medals can be awarded, because the order of the
   top three matters.

   Cross-check: npr(n, r) = n! / (n - r)! is the standard formula, and
   npr(n, r) = n * npr(n - 1, r - 1) builds the count one seat at a time.
   Both identities are verified below with exact integer arithmetic.

   Source: Ross, "A First Course in Probability", chapter 1. The counts
   are exact integers.

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/probability/counting/permutations.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\probability\counting\permutations.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/probability/counting/permutations.epher
   Web/desktop:             paste the file into the entry, press Enter

   The knob to edit is `runners`; everything below it is machinery.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the knob ----
const runners = 10

// ---- results ----
// The medals problem: the top three of `runners`, in order.
print("medal orders for", runners, "runners:", npr(runners, 3))
print("podium top two in order:", npr(runners, 2))

// More ordered counts from everyday settings.
print("3 distinct offices among 8 members:", npr(8, 3))
print("4-digit codes from 10 distinct digits:", npr(10, 4))
print("3-letter words from 5 distinct letters:", npr(5, 3))

// The standard formula npr(n, r) = n! / (n - r)!: an exact equality.
npr(runners, 3) == fact(runners) / fact(runners - 3)
npr(10, 4) == fact(10) / fact(6)

// Seat-by-seat building: 10 choices for gold, then npr(9, 2) for the
// next two seats, and the product is the same count.
10 * npr(9, 2) == npr(10, 3)

// The same race with more finishers.
print("with 12 runners, 3 medals:", npr(12, 3))
/* ---- expected output ----
= 10
= medal orders for 10 runners: 720
= podium top two in order: 90
= 3 distinct offices among 8 members: 336
= 4-digit codes from 10 distinct digits: 5040
= 3-letter words from 5 distinct letters: 60
= true
= true
= true
= with 12 runners, 3 medals: 1320
*/
